module tecs.watch

--[[
Watches content for changes and hands settled ones to reload handlers.

```nupp
local files <const> = require("tecs.files")
local watch <const> = require("tecs.watch")

watch.on("shader", function(change: watch.Change): nil
    print("reload " .. change.path)
end)
watch.add(files.assetPath("shaders/lit.wgsl"))
watch.install({intervalSeconds = 0.25})
```

A host advances the watcher by calling `poll` once per turn and `dispatch` once
per logical update. `poll` looks at the watched paths when the interval has
elapsed and queues what has settled; `dispatch` runs the handlers over that
queue. Keeping the two apart is what stops a handler from running inside a
half-completed phase: a reload replaces a resource other systems are reading,
so it belongs at a point the frame has declared, not wherever a poll happened to
land.

## Settling

An editor commonly saves by truncating and rewriting, or by writing a temporary
file and renaming it over the target. A watcher that dispatched the first change
it saw would hand a reload handler half a file. So a change is dispatched only
after the same size and modification time have been observed `settle` extra
times, and a path that is missing, empty, or a directory when a scan lands is
treated as a save in progress rather than as a change.

A path that changes and changes back inside the settle window dispatches
nothing, because the bytes on disk are the bytes that were already there.

## What is watched

Every watched path is registered by name through `add`. The SDL implementation
instead adopted whatever the process had read, because its own `files.read`
recorded each path and the watcher polled that set. The rewrite reads content
through `nupp.io.files`, which records nothing and should not start, so the
caller that loaded a file is the one that says it wants the file watched. That
also removes the `root` bound the old configuration needed: a set nobody chose
had to be fenced, and a set named one path at a time does not.

## The queue

Settled changes wait in a bounded queue, and repeated changes to one path
coalesce while they wait. `capacity` bounds distinct pending paths; an overflow
is logged and left for a later scan rather than growing without limit. A handler
that raises reaches the log and does not stop the rest of the batch, because a
handler raising has said the file is not usable yet, which is the same answer as
waiting for the next save.

## Polling rather than platform notification

This polls `nupp.io.files.info` and does not use a platform change-notification
service. `notify`, wrapped as a fourth Rust service, was the alternative, and it
loses on three counts. A notification says a write happened, not that the writer
finished, so the settle policy above still has to stat the file and the service
buys nothing the poll does not already pay for. The watched set is what a game
has loaded, which is dozens to low hundreds of paths, and one
`nupp.io.files.info` measures at 1.0 microseconds on the machine this was
written on: a hundred paths at two polls a second costs 200 microseconds per
second, which is 0.02 percent of one core. And a notification arrives on a
thread the Lua virtual machine never created, so it would have to cross the same
drain-a-buffer seam the gamepad and audio services cross, for an answer that
still needs the stat.
]]

local files = require("nupp.io.files")
local log = require("tecs.log")
local time = require("nupp.time")

--- One settled content change.
export record Change
    --- Read-only. Names the changed path, exactly as it was registered.
    path: string

    --- Read-only. Names the content kind the path was registered under. The
    --- built-in loaders use `"image"`, `"sound"`, `"font"`, `"shader"`,
    --- `"model"` and `"document"`, which are the `tecs.assets` kind names.
    kind: string
end

--- Receives one settled change.
export type ChangeHandler = function(change: Change): nil

--- Reads one path's size and modification time.
---
--- A path that is missing, or that has become a directory, answers `-1, -1`
--- rather than raising: some editors save by renaming a temporary over the
--- target, so a scan landing in the gap has to read that as not settled.
export type Source = function(path: string): (integer, number)

--- Settings copied by `install`.
---
--- `intervalSeconds` sets the delay between polls and defaults to 0.5.
--- `settle` requires that many extra matching scans after a change is first
--- seen and defaults to 1, so one scan observes and the next confirms; zero
--- queues immediately. `capacity` bounds distinct pending paths and defaults to
--- 1024. `clock` reads seconds from a monotonic origin and defaults to
--- `nupp.time`, and `source` reads one path's state and defaults to
--- `nupp.io.files.info`. The last two exist for a test or a headless tool that
--- drives its own time and its own filesystem; a game supplies neither.
export type Config = {
    intervalSeconds: number?,
    settle: integer?,
    capacity: integer?,
    clock: (function(): number)?,
    source: Source?
}

--- Seconds between polls. Fast enough that a save is picked up before a hand
--- reaches the window, slow enough that the poll is not on a frame's path.
local DEFAULT_INTERVAL: number = 0.5

--- Scans a change must repeat before it is queued. One means a change is seen
--- twice, which is what makes a rewrite in progress wait.
local DEFAULT_SETTLE: integer = 1

--- Distinct pending paths the queue holds before it refuses more.
local DEFAULT_CAPACITY: integer = 1024

-- Compatibility surface. Content kind names are what a reload handler is
-- registered under and what the debug tools report, so they keep the spelling
-- the SDL implementation recorded and the spelling `tecs.assets` declares.
-- Adding a name here is safe; changing one is a migration, not a rename.

--- Read-only. Names the kind a file registered without one is watched as.
export const DOCUMENT = "document"

--- Read-only. Names the kind a shader source is watched as.
export const SHADER = "shader"

--- What a document's extension makes it instead.
---
--- The load path decides every other kind, because something asked for an image
--- as an image or for a document as a font's metrics. A registration that named
--- no kind cannot know what wanted the file, so this is the one place a suffix
--- decides, and it decides only what nothing named. `glsl` is what the SDL
--- implementation mapped; `wgsl` is beside it because the rewrite's shaders are
--- WGSL and a shader has to reload as a shader under both.
local DOCUMENT_KINDS: {[string]: string} = {glsl = SHADER, wgsl = SHADER}

--- One watched path, and how sure the watcher is about its current state.
local record Entry
    --- What kind this path reloads as.
    kind: string

    --- The state last accepted, which is what a change is measured against.
    size: integer

    stamp: number

    --- The state the last scan saw, and how many scans have agreed on it.
    pendingSize: integer

    pendingStamp: number
    steady: integer
end

local logger = log.get(log.WATCH)

local handlers: {[string]: ChangeHandler?} = {}
local entries: {[string]: Entry} = {}
local running: boolean = false
local interval: number = DEFAULT_INTERVAL
local settleCount: integer = DEFAULT_SETTLE
local capacity: integer = DEFAULT_CAPACITY
local lastPoll: number = 0
local dispatchedCount: integer = 0
local queue: {Change} = {}
local queued: {[string]: boolean} = {}

--- Reads one path through `nupp.io.files`, the default `Source`.
--- @param path the path to describe
--- @return the byte length, or -1 when nothing usable is there
--- @return seconds since the Unix epoch, or -1 when nothing usable is there
local function defaultSource(path: string): (integer, number)
    local info = files.info(path)
    if info == nil or info.kind ~= "file" then
        return -1, -1
    end

    return info.size, info.modified
end

--- Reads the monotonic clock in seconds, the default `Config.clock`.
--- @return seconds since this process's clock origin
local function defaultClock(): number
    return time.now() / 1000
end

local source: Source = defaultSource
local clock: function(): number = defaultClock

--- Returns what a path reloads as, given the kind it was registered under.
--- @param path the registered path
--- @param registered the kind the caller named, or the document kind
--- @return the kind a change to this path dispatches under
local function kindOf(path: string, registered: string): string
    if registered ~= DOCUMENT then
        return registered
    end
    local suffix = path:match("%.([%a%d]+)$")
    if suffix == nil then
        return DOCUMENT
    end

    return DOCUMENT_KINDS[suffix:lower()] or DOCUMENT
end

--- Registers a path to watch, and reports whether it was newly registered.
---
--- The path's current state becomes the baseline, so a file registered on frame
--- ten does not dispatch on frame eleven. Registering a path already watched
--- moves its kind and leaves its baseline alone, which is what a second loader
--- naming the same file should do.
---
--- Registration does not require an installed watcher. A loader registers what
--- it loaded whenever it loads it, and `install` takes the whole set up.
---
--- @param path the file to watch
--- @param kind the content kind a change dispatches under, defaulting to
---     `"document"` and to `"shader"` for a `.glsl` or `.wgsl` suffix
--- @return whether this call added the path rather than updating it
--- @raises when path is empty
export function add(path: string, kind: string?): boolean
    if path == "" then
        error("tecs.watch.add: a path is required", 2)
    end
    local named = kindOf(path, kind or DOCUMENT)
    local held = entries[path]
    if held ~= nil then
        held.kind = named

        return false
    end
    local size, stamp = source(path)
    entries[path] = new Entry(kind = named, size = size, stamp = stamp, pendingSize = -1, pendingStamp = -1, steady = 0)

    return true
end

--- Stops watching one path and forgets its state.
---
--- A change already queued for the path still dispatches, because it settled
--- while the path was watched and a handler that has been promised a reload
--- gets one.
---
--- @param path the file to stop watching
--- @return whether the path was being watched
export function forget(path: string): boolean
    if entries[path] == nil then
        return false
    end
    entries[path] = nil

    return true
end

--- Registers the handler for a content kind.
---
--- Re-registering a kind replaces its handler, and nil removes it. Handlers
--- outlive `install` and `uninstall`, so a build that stops and starts watching
--- registers them once.
---
--- @param kind the content kind to register under
--- @param handler the handler to run, or nil to remove the current one
--- @raises when kind is empty
export function on(kind: string, handler: ChangeHandler?): nil
    if kind == "" then
        error("tecs.watch.on: a handler needs a kind to be registered under", 2)
    end
    handlers[kind] = handler
end

--- Returns the content kinds that have a handler, in sorted order.
--- @return a fresh caller-owned list
export function kinds(): {string}
    local names: {string} = {}
    for kind, handler in pairs(handlers) do
        if handler ~= nil then
            names[#names + 1] = kind
        end
    end
    table.sort(names)

    return names
end

--- Returns the watched paths, in sorted order.
--- @return a fresh caller-owned list
export function watching(): {string}
    local list: {string} = {}
    for path in pairs(entries) do
        list[#list + 1] = path
    end
    table.sort(list)

    return list
end

--- Returns the watched paths whose latest change has not settled, sorted.
--- @return a fresh caller-owned list
export function unsettled(): {string}
    local list: {string} = {}
    for path, entry in pairs(entries) do
        if entry.pendingSize >= 0 then
            list[#list + 1] = path
        end
    end
    table.sort(list)

    return list
end

--- Returns how many changes have reached a handler since `install`.
--- @return the cumulative count of handler calls that returned
export function dispatched(): integer
    return dispatchedCount
end

--- Returns how many settled changes are waiting for `dispatch`.
--- @return the number of distinct queued paths
export function pending(): integer
    return #queue
end

--- Returns whether the watcher is running.
--- @return true after `install` and before `uninstall`
export function isInstalled(): boolean
    return running
end

--- Starts watching, taking every registered path's current state as its
--- baseline.
---
--- Changes made before this call do not dispatch, which is what lets a build
--- register paths as it loads them and install once everything is loaded.
---
--- @param config the polling options, or nil for the documented defaults
--- @raises when settle is negative or capacity is not a positive integer
export function install(config: Config?): nil
    local given = config
    interval = given ~= nil and given.intervalSeconds or DEFAULT_INTERVAL
    settleCount = given ~= nil and given.settle or DEFAULT_SETTLE
    capacity = given ~= nil and given.capacity or DEFAULT_CAPACITY
    if settleCount < 0 then
        error("tecs.watch.install: settle counts scans, so it cannot be negative", 2)
    end
    if capacity < 1 or capacity % 1 ~= 0 then
        error("tecs.watch.install: capacity must be a positive integer", 2)
    end
    source = given ~= nil and given.source or defaultSource
    clock = given ~= nil and given.clock or defaultClock
    queue = {}
    queued = {}
    dispatchedCount = 0
    for path, entry in pairs(entries) do
        local size, stamp = source(path)
        entry.size = size
        entry.stamp = stamp
        entry.pendingSize = -1
        entry.pendingStamp = -1
        entry.steady = 0
    end
    running = true
    lastPoll = clock()
end

--- Stops watching and drops whatever was queued.
---
--- Registered paths and handlers survive, so a later `install` resumes with the
--- same set and takes a fresh baseline.
export function uninstall(): nil
    running = false
    queue = {}
    queued = {}
end

--- Queues one settled change, and reports whether the queue took it.
--- @param path the changed path
--- @param entry the watched path's state
--- @return whether the change is now queued
local function enqueue(path: string, entry: Entry): boolean
    if queued[path] then
        return true
    end
    if #queue >= capacity then
        logger:error("the watch queue is full; retaining '%s' for a later scan", path)

        return false
    end
    queue[#queue + 1] = new Change(path = path, kind = entry.kind)
    queued[path] = true

    return true
end

--- Looks at every watched path once, whatever the interval says.
---
--- Call this directly when a headless tool or a test controls the timing. A
--- change that has now settled is queued rather than dispatched, because the
--- handler runs where the frame says it runs.
---
--- @return how many changes this scan queued, and zero when nothing is running
export function scan(): integer
    if not running then
        return 0
    end
    local settled: integer = 0
    for path, entry in pairs(entries) do
        local size, stamp = source(path)
        if size <= 0 then
            -- Missing, a directory, or empty: a save in progress rather than a
            -- state to reload from. The next scan looks again.
            entry.pendingSize = -1
            entry.pendingStamp = -1
            entry.steady = 0
        elseif size ~= entry.size or stamp ~= entry.stamp then
            if size == entry.pendingSize and stamp == entry.pendingStamp then
                entry.steady = entry.steady + 1
            else
                entry.pendingSize = size
                entry.pendingStamp = stamp
                entry.steady = 0
            end
            if entry.steady >= settleCount and enqueue(path, entry) then
                entry.size = size
                entry.stamp = stamp
                entry.pendingSize = -1
                entry.pendingStamp = -1
                entry.steady = 0
                settled = settled + 1
            end
        elseif entry.pendingSize >= 0 then
            -- Changed and then changed back inside the settle window, which is
            -- a save that produced the bytes that were already there.
            entry.pendingSize = -1
            entry.pendingStamp = -1
            entry.steady = 0
        end
    end

    return settled
end

--- Scans when the interval has elapsed, and does nothing otherwise.
---
--- This is what a host calls once per turn. It never dispatches, so it is safe
--- anywhere in a turn.
---
--- @return how many changes the scan queued, and zero when it was too early
export function poll(): integer
    if not running then
        return 0
    end
    local now = clock()
    if now - lastPoll < interval then
        return 0
    end
    lastPoll = now

    return scan()
end

--- Runs the registered handlers over every queued change.
---
--- Call this once per logical update, at a point the frame has declared. A
--- handler that raises is logged and the batch continues, because one file that
--- is not usable yet says nothing about the next.
---
--- @return how many handlers ran and returned
export function dispatch(): integer
    local batch = queue
    if #batch == 0 then
        return 0
    end
    queue = {}
    queued = {}
    local fired: integer = 0
    for index = 1, #batch do
        local change = batch[index]
        local handler = handlers[change.kind]
        if handler ~= nil then
            local succeeded, failure = pcall(function(): nil
                handler(change)
            end)
            if succeeded then
                logger:info("reloaded '%s' as %s", change.path, change.kind)
                fired = fired + 1
            else
                logger:error("reloading '%s' failed: %s", change.path, tostring(failure))
            end
        end
    end
    dispatchedCount = dispatchedCount + fired

    return fired
end

--- Forgets every path, handler and queued change, and stops watching.
---
--- A test that installs the watcher needs this between cases. A game does not:
--- `uninstall` already stops the polling and keeps what was registered.
export function reset(): nil
    running = false
    handlers = {}
    entries = {}
    queue = {}
    queued = {}
    dispatchedCount = 0
    interval = DEFAULT_INTERVAL
    settleCount = DEFAULT_SETTLE
    capacity = DEFAULT_CAPACITY
    source = defaultSource
    clock = defaultClock
    lastPoll = 0
end
